## 3. Independently verified fact base **[EXTERNAL FACT]** (capture identities in sources.json)

**F-01 — tifffile v2026.9.20 (README, captured 2026-10-06T05:41:38Z, sha256 `cdd8ac84…`, version string "2026.9.20").** (a) "Image data can be read as NumPy arrays or Zarr arrays/groups from strips, tiles, pages (IFDs), SubIFDs, higher-order series, and pyramidal levels" — the mechanism for bounded, chunked access to multi-GB TIFF-family sources. (b) Revision 2026.5.2: "Update ZarrTiffStore to zarr format 3 and multiscales to NGFF 0.5 (breaking)"; "Add return_as parameter to imread; deprecate aszarr". (c) Revision 2026.9.9: "Fix TiffSeries(squeeze=None) incorrectly squeezes 'shaped' series (breaking)". (d) Notes section states an explicit unsupported set (OJPEG, chroma subsampling without JPEG, color-space transformations, differing sample types, ICC/IPTC/XMP "are not implemented") and that Micro-Manager MMStack metadata "are often corrupted or wrong" — a working model for writing a support boundary and for ambiguous-metadata handling. (e) Release notes name issue-numbered fixes (#334 in 2026.9.15, #327/#328 in 2026.6.1) — release-note level only; full chains not verified (Lead L-01). Delivery limit: 32,768 of 32,898 bytes delivered (tail truncation recorded).

**F-02 — OME-Zarr (NGFF) 0.5 specification (ngff.openmicroscopy.org/0.5/index.html, captured 2026-10-06T05:42:30Z, sha256 `a57e3bf8…`, Last-Modified 2026-10-01; spec text read at bytes 65536–95536, range sha256 `127f7a3e…`).** (a) §2.3 `"coordinateTransformations"`: each entry's `type` MUST be one of **`identity`, `translation`, `scale`** (per the 0.5 type table), and "The transformations in the list are applied sequentially and in order." — **no rotation/shear/affine type exists in 0.5**. (b) §2.1 `"axes"`: name MUST be present and unique; type SHOULD be space/time/channel; unit SHOULD be UDUNITS-2; axes length MUST equal array dimensionality; `dimension_names` MUST be present in array `zarr.json` and match axes names. (c) §2.4 `"multiscales"`: 2–5 dimensions; axis entries MUST be ordered time → channel/custom → space; "zyx" SHOULD ordering for anisotropic stacking. (d) Storage uses Zarr format 3 (`zarr.json`, `zarr_format: 3`); OME metadata version `"0.5"` under `attributes.ome`. **Consequence:** any Brief-A plan that assumes a public standard transform can carry arbitrary orientation (flips/permutations/rotations) cannot cite NGFF 0.5 for that; the project's own metadata must carry it (see I-01, D-01).

**F-03 — SQLite "How To Corrupt An SQLite Database File" (sqlite.org/howtocorrupt.html, captured 2026-10-06T05:42:31Z, sha256 `e00709e3…`, Last-Modified 2026-09-11).** (a) Overview: crash/power-failure mid-transaction "should be automatically rolled back the next time the database file is accessed" — the mechanism behind a crash-atomic single-file project DB option. (b) §2.1: locking bugs "especially true of network filesystems and NFS in particular" — the hazard for project DBs living on ordinary shared storage with multi-writer access. (c) §1.4: "Copying a database file without also copying its journal" can corrupt — bundle-export must copy quiescent DBs or use `VACUUM INTO`/backup API. (d) §3.1/§3.2: WAL-mode sync failures lose durability but not consistency; `synchronous=FULL` recommended. Read range: first 32,768 bytes of 46,737 (TOC + §§1–3 covered).

**F-04 — RFC 7946 GeoJSON (rfc-editor.org, captured 2026-10-06T05:43:25Z, sha256 `cb52f07b…`).** (a) §3.1.1 position: longitude/latitude "precisely in that order"; "SHOULD NOT extend positions beyond three elements because the semantics of extra elements are unspecified and ambiguous". (b) §4: CRS is fixed to WGS 84; alternative-CRS support "has been removed from this version … because … [it] has proven to have interoperability issues" — a standards-level cautionary tale for export designs that silently reinterpret coordinates. (c) §6.1 foreign members are allowed but "support … can vary across implementations" — export honesty must call out which members are non-standard. (d) §3.1.6 ring winding with backward-compat "parsers SHOULD NOT reject". Read range: first 32,768 of 49,143 bytes.

**F-05 — Negative captures (URL rot is real).** `raw.githubusercontent.com/ome/ngff/main/0.5/index.md` → 404 (sha256 `d5558cd4…`); `qupath.readthedocs.io/en/stable/docs/geo/geojson.html` → 404 (sha256 `d2c42d8f…`). **Consequence:** every cited URL in a proposal must be captured; "stable-looking" doc URLs fail.

## 4. Executed checks **[EXECUTED BY CANDIDATE]** — receipts in witnesses.json

**W1 — calibration precision (exec-suyktz8b, exit 0, code sha256 `9eee5a45…`).** Physical coordinate `origin + k·dx` with stage offset 12,345.6 µm, pixel size 0.107 µm, k=100,000: storing/computing operands through float32 shifts the result to 23,045.599609375 µm (absolute error ≈ 0.00039 µm ≈ 0.39 nm); float64 arithmetic reproduces 23,045.6 exactly in this arithmetic. Scope limits: pure IEEE-754 arithmetic in an isolated sandbox; it does not validate any library, file format, or application; the demonstrated magnitude (sub-nanometre here) is scenario-dependent — its value is that the precision requirement is *checkable*, not that float32 always fails.

**W2 — transform representability (same receipt) — ADVERSE RESULT, reported as such.** I intended to show a per-axis (diagonal) scale+origin model cannot represent a flip+axis-swap while a full 2×2 affine can. The executed check shows the opposite for my constructed case: the diagonal model matched the manual mapping (`scalar_matches_manual: true`) and my affine did not (`affine_matches_manual: false`) — my affine was mis-specified (it encoded no swap) and a diagonal per-axis map with a negative scale plus output-axis relabeling *can* express single-axis flips and swaps. **Correct surviving statement:** diagonal per-axis maps cannot express *rotation/shear* (genuine mixing of two input axes into one output axis); and independently of my arithmetic, **F-02(a)** shows the public standard NGFF 0.5 has no affine transform type at all — so orientation/rotation must live in project-owned metadata. A corrected discriminating check remains **PROPOSED/UNEXECUTED**. Also recorded: the first execution attempt (exec-1dhsi1mt) failed with SyntaxError, exit 1 (receipt retained). Lesson for the next candidate: an executed check can refute its own intended claim — read the stdout against the expectation before citing it.

